Chapter: CryptoBig

 Big unsigned integers for public-key cryptography: enough to raise a
 2048-bit number to a power modulo another one, and nothing else.

 THIS IS NOT `Foreword chapter BigInt` AND MUST NOT BECOME IT. BigInt
 stores base-10000 limbs so that printing a decimal number is cheap, and
 its division is `bigint-div-slow`, which subtracts the divisor one at a
 time. For decimal arithmetic on human-sized numbers that is a reasonable
 trade. For RSA it is not slow, it is impossible: reducing a 4096-bit
 product modulo a 2048-bit modulus would take about 2^2048 subtractions.
 A cryptographic modexp needs a different representation and a different
 reduction, so it gets its own chapter rather than a flag on that one.

 The base is 2^16. Two reasons, and both are about not lying to
 ourselves. A limb pair is exactly one byte pair, so a certificate's
 big-endian INTEGER converts to limbs and back with no arithmetic at all
 -- base 10000 would need a division per digit and a rounding question at
 every boundary. And the widest intermediate here is
 `limb + limb*limb + carry`, which is under 2^33, so every step fits in a
 64-bit Integer with room to spare and there is no overflow to reason
 about.

 Reduction is Montgomery's, not division, because division is the thing
 we cannot afford. The cost of that choice is that values must be carried
 in Montgomery form between multiplications, which is why `cb-mod-exp`
 converts in at the start and out at the end and nothing in between does.

 We say:

Section: Representation

 A number is a List Integer of limbs, least significant first, each in
 [0, 65536), and always exactly as long as the modulus. Fixed width means
 no function has to decide what a shorter operand means.

  cb-base : Integer = 65536
  cb-mask : Integer = 65535
  cb-shift : Integer = 16

  cb-zeros : Integer -> List Integer
  cb-zeros (n) = cb-zeros-loop n 0 []

  cb-zeros-loop : Integer, Integer, List Integer -> List Integer
  cb-zeros-loop (n) (i) (acc) =
    if i >= n then acc
    else cb-zeros-loop n (i + 1) (list-push acc 0)

  cb-one : Integer -> List Integer
  cb-one (n) = list-set-at (cb-zeros n) 0 1

 Big-endian bytes in, limbs out, padded or truncated to n limbs. Bytes
 are walked from the END, two at a time, which is what makes this
 conversion exact: the low byte of a limb is the later byte on the wire.

  cb-from-bytes : List Integer, Integer -> List Integer
  cb-from-bytes (bs) (n) = cb-fb-loop bs (list-length bs - 1) 0 (cb-zeros n) n

  cb-fb-loop : List Integer, Integer, Integer, List Integer, Integer -> List Integer
  cb-fb-loop (bs) (bi) (li) (acc) (n) =
    if li >= n then acc
    else if bi < 0 then acc
    else cb-fb-step bs bi li acc n

  cb-fb-step : List Integer, Integer, Integer, List Integer, Integer -> List Integer
  cb-fb-step (bs) (bi) (li) (acc) (n) =
    let lo = list-at bs bi
    in let hi = if bi - 1 >= 0 then list-at bs (bi - 1) else 0
    in cb-fb-loop bs (bi - 2) (li + 1) (list-set-at acc li (lo + hi * 256)) n

 Limbs out, big-endian bytes in, exactly nbytes of them. A signature and
 an encoded message are both fixed-width byte strings, so the width is
 the caller's and never inferred from how large the number happens to be.

  cb-to-bytes : List Integer, Integer -> List Integer
  cb-to-bytes (x) (nbytes) = cb-tb-loop x nbytes 0 []

  cb-tb-loop : List Integer, Integer, Integer, List Integer -> List Integer
  cb-tb-loop (x) (nbytes) (i) (acc) =
    if i >= nbytes then acc
    else cb-tb-step x nbytes i acc

  cb-tb-step : List Integer, Integer, Integer, List Integer -> List Integer
  cb-tb-step (x) (nbytes) (i) (acc) =
    let p = nbytes - 1 - i
    in let li = p / 2
    in let limb = if li < list-length x then list-at x li else 0
    in let b = if int-rem p 2 == 0 then bit-and limb 255 else bit-and (bit-shru limb 8) 255
    in cb-tb-loop x nbytes (i + 1) (list-push acc b)

Section: Comparison And Subtraction

  cb-cmp : List Integer, List Integer, Integer -> Integer
  cb-cmp (a) (b) (n) = cb-cmp-loop a b (n - 1)

  cb-cmp-loop : List Integer, List Integer, Integer -> Integer
  cb-cmp-loop (a) (b) (i) =
    if i < 0 then 0
    else if list-at a i > list-at b i then 1
    else if list-at a i < list-at b i then -1
    else cb-cmp-loop a b (i - 1)

 a := a - b, in place, assuming a >= b. The borrow is explicit because a
 limb that goes negative must carry the borrow up rather than clamp: a
 clamped borrow is the classic silent-wrong-answer in bignum subtraction.

  cb-sub-into : List Integer, List Integer, Integer -> List Integer
  cb-sub-into (a) (b) (n) = cb-sub-loop a b n 0 0

  cb-sub-loop : List Integer, List Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer -> List Integer
  cb-sub-loop (a) (b) (n) (i) (borrow) =
    if i >= n then a
    else cb-sub-step a b n i borrow (list-at a i - list-at b i - borrow)

  cb-sub-step : List Integer, List Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer -> List Integer
  cb-sub-step (a) (b) (n) (i) (borrow) (d) =
    if d < 0 then cb-sub-loop (list-set-at a i (d + cb-base)) b n (i + 1) 1
    else cb-sub-loop (list-set-at a i d) b n (i + 1) 0

Section: The Montgomery Constant

 n0inv is -m[0] inverse modulo 2^16. Newton's iteration doubles the
 number of correct bits each time, so starting from 1 (correct modulo 2)
 five rounds are more than the four needed to reach 16 bits.

  cb-n0inv : List Integer -> Integer
  cb-n0inv (m) = cb-n0inv-neg (cb-newton (list-at m 0) 1 0)

  cb-newton : Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  cb-newton (n0) (inv) (k) =
    if k >= 5 then inv
    else cb-newton n0 (bit-and (inv * (2 - n0 * inv)) cb-mask) (k + 1)

  cb-n0inv-neg : Integer -> Integer
  cb-n0inv-neg (inv) = bit-and (cb-base - inv) cb-mask

Section: Montgomery Multiplication

 CIOS: for each limb of b, add a*b[i] into the accumulator, then add a
 multiple of the modulus chosen to clear the bottom limb, then shift one
 limb down. The accumulator is n+2 limbs because the running value can
 exceed the modulus by two limbs before the shift.

 The accumulator is mutated in place through list-set-at. That is the
 concession this chapter needs and the reason it is threaded through
 every helper rather than rebuilt: at 128 limbs and 34 multiplications a
 fresh list per limb-step would allocate tens of megabytes to compute one
 signature check.

  cb-mont-mul : List Integer, List Integer, List Integer, Integer, Integer -> List Integer
  cb-mont-mul (a) (b) (m) (n) (n0inv) =
    cb-mont-outer a b m n n0inv (cb-zeros (n + 2)) 0

  cb-mont-outer : List Integer, List Integer, List Integer, Integer, Integer, List Integer, Integer -> List Integer
  cb-mont-outer (a) (b) (m) (n) (n0inv) (t) (i) =
    if i >= n then cb-mont-final t m n
    else cb-mont-round a b m n n0inv t i

  cb-mont-round : List Integer, List Integer, List Integer, Integer, Integer, List Integer, Integer -> List Integer
  cb-mont-round (a) (b) (m) (n) (n0inv) (t) (i) =
    let t1 = cb-mul-add t a (list-at b i) n 0 0
    in let mu = bit-and ((list-at t1 0) * n0inv) cb-mask
    in let t2 = cb-red-add t1 m mu n 0 0
    in cb-mont-outer a b m n n0inv (cb-shift-down t2 n 0) (i + 1)

  cb-mul-add : List Integer, List Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer -> List Integer
  cb-mul-add (t) (a) (bi) (n) (j) (carry) =
    if j >= n then cb-mul-add-tail t n carry
    else cb-mul-add-step t a bi n j ((list-at t j) + (list-at a j) * bi + carry)

  cb-mul-add-step : List Integer, List Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer -> List Integer
  cb-mul-add-step (t) (a) (bi) (n) (j) (v) =
    cb-mul-add (list-set-at t j (bit-and v cb-mask)) a bi n (j + 1) (bit-shru v cb-shift)

  cb-mul-add-tail : List Integer, Integer, Integer -> List Integer
  cb-mul-add-tail (t) (n) (carry) =
    let v = (list-at t n) + carry
    in list-set-at (list-set-at t n (bit-and v cb-mask)) (n + 1) ((list-at t (n + 1)) + (bit-shru v cb-shift))

 t := t + mu * m, which by the choice of mu leaves t[0] zero.

  cb-red-add : List Integer, List Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer -> List Integer
  cb-red-add (t) (m) (mu) (n) (j) (carry) =
    if j >= n then cb-red-add-tail t n carry
    else cb-red-add-step t m mu n j ((list-at t j) + (list-at m j) * mu + carry)

  cb-red-add-step : List Integer, List Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer -> List Integer
  cb-red-add-step (t) (m) (mu) (n) (j) (v) =
    cb-red-add (list-set-at t j (bit-and v cb-mask)) m mu n (j + 1) (bit-shru v cb-shift)

  cb-red-add-tail : List Integer, Integer, Integer -> List Integer
  cb-red-add-tail (t) (n) (carry) =
    let v = (list-at t n) + carry
    in list-set-at (list-set-at t n (bit-and v cb-mask)) (n + 1) ((list-at t (n + 1)) + (bit-shru v cb-shift))

 One limb down. t[0] is zero by construction, so dropping it is the
 division by 2^16 that Montgomery reduction is named for.

  cb-shift-down : List Integer, Integer, Integer -> List Integer
  cb-shift-down (t) (n) (j) =
    if j > n then list-set-at t (n + 1) 0
    else cb-shift-down (list-set-at t j (list-at t (j + 1))) n (j + 1)

 The result is under twice the modulus, so at most one subtraction is
 needed. The spare limb is checked as well as the comparison: a value
 that overflowed into it is larger than the modulus however the low
 limbs compare.

  cb-mont-final : List Integer, List Integer, Integer -> List Integer
  cb-mont-final (t) (m) (n) =
    let r = cb-take t n
    in if (list-at t n) > 0 then cb-sub-into r m n
       else if cb-cmp r m n >= 0 then cb-sub-into r m n
       else r

  cb-take : List Integer, Integer -> List Integer
  cb-take (t) (n) = cb-take-loop t n 0 []

  cb-take-loop : List Integer, Integer, Integer, List Integer -> List Integer
  cb-take-loop (t) (n) (i) (acc) =
    if i >= n then acc
    else cb-take-loop t n (i + 1) (list-push acc (list-at t i))

Section: R Squared

 Montgomery form needs R^2 mod m, and computing it is the one place a
 division would otherwise be required. It is avoided by doubling: start
 at 1 and double 32n times modulo m, which is 2^(32n) = R^2. Each
 doubling is a shift and at most one subtraction, so the whole thing is
 linear in the modulus size and needs no reduction primitive.

  cb-r-squared : List Integer, Integer -> List Integer
  cb-r-squared (m) (n) = cb-r2-loop (cb-one n) m n 0 (32 * n)

  cb-r2-loop : List Integer, List Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer -> List Integer
  cb-r2-loop (x) (m) (n) (k) (rounds) =
    if k >= rounds then x
    else cb-r2-loop (cb-dbl-mod x m n) m n (k + 1) rounds

  cb-dbl-mod : List Integer, List Integer, Integer -> List Integer
  cb-dbl-mod (x) (m) (n) = cb-dbl-fix x m n (cb-shl1 x n 0 0)

  cb-dbl-fix : List Integer, List Integer, Integer, Integer -> List Integer
  cb-dbl-fix (x) (m) (n) (carry) =
    if carry > 0 then cb-sub-into x m n
    else if cb-cmp x m n >= 0 then cb-sub-into x m n
    else x

  cb-shl1 : List Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  cb-shl1 (x) (n) (i) (carry) =
    if i >= n then carry
    else cb-shl1-step x n i ((list-at x i) * 2 + carry)

  cb-shl1-step : List Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  cb-shl1-step (x) (n) (i) (v) =
    cb-shl1 (list-set-at x i (bit-and v cb-mask)) n (i + 1) (bit-shru v cb-shift)

Section: Modular Exponentiation

 base^exp mod m, every operand a big-endian byte string, the answer a
 byte string as wide as the modulus. Square and multiply over the
 exponent's bits, most significant first, in Montgomery form throughout.

 The exponent is NOT secret here and this is NOT constant time. That is
 honest for what this chapter is for: verifying a signature with a public
 exponent against a public key. Nothing private goes through it, and if
 anything private ever does, this comment is the thing that was wrong.

  cb-mod-exp : List Integer, List Integer, List Integer -> List Integer
  cb-mod-exp (base-bytes) (exp-bytes) (mod-bytes) =
    cb-exp-setup base-bytes exp-bytes mod-bytes ((list-length mod-bytes + 1) / 2)

  cb-exp-setup : List Integer, List Integer, List Integer, Integer -> List Integer
  cb-exp-setup (base-bytes) (exp-bytes) (mod-bytes) (n) =
    let m = cb-from-bytes mod-bytes n
    in cb-exp-go (cb-from-bytes base-bytes n) exp-bytes m n (cb-n0inv m) (cb-r-squared m n)

  cb-exp-go : List Integer, List Integer, List Integer, Integer, Integer, List Integer -> List Integer
  cb-exp-go (a) (exp-bytes) (m) (n) (n0inv) (r2) =
    let a-mont = cb-mont-mul a r2 m n n0inv
    in let one-mont = cb-mont-mul (cb-one n) r2 m n n0inv
    in cb-exp-out (cb-exp-bits a-mont one-mont exp-bytes m n n0inv (cb-top-bit exp-bytes)) m n n0inv

  cb-exp-out : List Integer, List Integer, Integer, Integer -> List Integer
  cb-exp-out (acc) (m) (n) (n0inv) = cb-mont-mul acc (cb-one n) m n n0inv

 The index of the highest set bit, or -1 for a zero exponent. Starting
 the square-and-multiply below the top bit would square in leading zeros,
 which is harmless but slow; starting above it is what makes an exponent
 of zero answer one.

  cb-top-bit : List Integer -> Integer
  cb-top-bit (bs) = cb-top-loop bs (list-length bs * 8 - 1)

  cb-top-loop : List Integer, Integer -> Integer
  cb-top-loop (bs) (i) =
    if i < 0 then -1
    else if cb-bit-at bs i == 1 then i
    else cb-top-loop bs (i - 1)

  cb-bit-at : List Integer, Integer -> Integer
  cb-bit-at (bs) (i) =
    let byte-from-end = i / 8
    in let idx = list-length bs - 1 - byte-from-end
    in if idx < 0 then 0
       else bit-and (bit-shru (list-at bs idx) (int-rem i 8)) 1

  cb-exp-bits : List Integer, List Integer, List Integer, List Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer -> List Integer
  cb-exp-bits (a-mont) (acc) (exp-bytes) (m) (n) (n0inv) (i) =
    if i < 0 then acc
    else cb-exp-step a-mont (cb-mont-mul acc acc m n n0inv) exp-bytes m n n0inv i

  cb-exp-step : List Integer, List Integer, List Integer, List Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer -> List Integer
  cb-exp-step (a-mont) (sq) (exp-bytes) (m) (n) (n0inv) (i) =
    if cb-bit-at exp-bytes i == 1
    then cb-exp-bits a-mont (cb-mont-mul sq a-mont m n n0inv) exp-bytes m n n0inv (i - 1)
    else cb-exp-bits a-mont sq exp-bytes m n n0inv (i - 1)

  cb-mod-exp-bytes : List Integer, List Integer, List Integer -> List Integer
  cb-mod-exp-bytes (base-bytes) (exp-bytes) (mod-bytes) =
    cb-to-bytes (cb-mod-exp base-bytes exp-bytes mod-bytes) (list-length mod-bytes)

